<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/financial-statements">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Financial statements are <strong>a company's three-part self-portrait</strong>: the income
    statement (performance over time), the balance sheet (position at an instant), and the cash
    flow statement (where money actually moved). Read separately they mislead; read together they
    reveal the business — its model, its health, and sometimes its crimes.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three statements and how they interlock" widget="equationStep" config={stmtsCfg} />

  <h2>The ratios that do the work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Profitability:</strong> gross margin (pricing power), operating margin (operating efficiency), ROE (return on owners' capital — with the caveat that leverage inflates it).</li>
    <li><strong>Liquidity & leverage:</strong> current ratio (can it pay short-term bills?), debt-to-equity (how aggressive is the structure?). High leverage amplifies both good years and fatal ones.</li>
    <li><strong>Cash conversion:</strong> operating cash flow ÷ net income — persistently below 1 suggests "profitable" on paper only. Enron passed every accrual test; cash told the truth.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch the footnotes:</strong> accounting lives in estimates (depreciation, reserves, revenue timing). The notes are where the estimates hide — professionals read them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Graham & Dodd, Security Analysis; IFRS/GAAP frameworks; Penman, Financial Statement Analysis and Security Valuation.</p>
</TopicPage>
